<Layout title="Privacy policy · OpenOrc" description="How the OpenOrc website, desktop app, and iPhone and iPad app handle information, including local storage, model providers, the phone’s encrypted link to your Mac, and optional integrations.">
  <SiteHeader />
  <main id="main" class="container prose-page">
    <header class="prose-intro">
      <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="prose-date">Last updated November 25, 2026</p>
      <p>This page describes what information the OpenOrc website, the OpenOrc desktop app, the OpenOrc app for iPhone and iPad, and OpenOrc Cloud handle.</p>
    </header>
    <article class="prose-article" aria-label="Privacy policy">
      <section aria-labelledby="website-title">
        <h2 id="website-title">This website</h2>
        <p>This website is a set of static pages with no accounts or forms. It uses Google Analytics to measure visits and which pages people use. Your browser loads Google's measurement script and sends information about your visit to Google Analytics, including the page viewed and browser and device details. Google Analytics uses first-party cookies to distinguish visitors and sessions. You can read more about this in <a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/6004245">Google's description of Analytics data collection</a>.</p>
        <p>Like any website, the server that delivers these pages receives the information included in every web request, such as your IP address, the page requested, and your browser’s user agent. The hosting service may keep this in access logs under its own terms.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="app-title">
        <h2 id="app-title">The desktop app</h2>
        <p>OpenOrc stores its settings, conversation history, tasks, execution events, and project memory on your computer. Project files and Git worktrees stay in their own folders. The desktop app works without an account and sends no usage analytics. OpenOrc Cloud, described below, is optional and is the only part of OpenOrc with an account.</p>
        <p>When an agent runs, its provider receives your request and the context the agent works with, which can include conversation history, project files, and tool output. Providers handle this under their own terms and your account settings. The agent command-line tools you install also connect to their own services and keep their own local transcripts, under their own terms.</p>
        <p>To name a conversation, OpenOrc sends the first 1,500 characters of your first request and of the reply to a small model from the conversation’s own agent. You can choose another provider for titles, or turn them off, in settings.</p>
        <p>When you ask OpenOrc to explain a code file, it sends the file’s path and text to the same kind of small model, from the conversation’s agent or the provider you chose for titles, and keeps the explanation on your computer. Turning titles off also turns explanations off.</p>
        <p>Memory is off until you turn it on. While it is on, OpenOrc summarizes finished runs to find what is worth remembering, and adds saved memories to later requests, whichever agent runs them. By default the agent that did the work also summarizes it. OpenCode cannot summarize yet, so its runs are skipped. You can instead choose one provider, or your Anthropic API key, to summarize every eligible run, including OpenCode’s. Replies to task comments are never summarized.</p>
        <p>Memory search uses a 91 MB embedding model that runs on your computer. OpenOrc downloads it from Hugging Face the first time memory needs it: when it saves a memory, searches, or indexes saved memories at startup. Each file is checked against a fixed SHA-256 checksum. Nothing is downloaded while memory is off.</p>
        <p>Dictation turns what you say into text with a 731 MB speech model that runs on your computer. OpenOrc downloads the model from Hugging Face only when you choose Download, and checks it against a fixed SHA-256 checksum. The microphone is on only while you dictate. Your voice is not saved and does not leave your computer; the text goes to your agent when you send it, like anything you type. If you instead choose OpenAI for dictation in settings and add your own API key, what you say while dictating is sent to OpenAI to be transcribed, under the terms of your API account, and is not saved by OpenOrc. The key is kept in your operating system’s protected storage.</p>
        <p>Installed releases ask GitHub Releases for updates shortly after launch and every six hours. These checks send no identifier for your installation, and you can turn them off in settings. Downloading and installing an update always requires your action.</p>
        <p>On the same schedule, OpenOrc checks whether the agent command-line tools you installed have newer versions. It asks <code>registry.npmjs.org</code>, <code>formulae.brew.sh</code> for tools installed with Homebrew, or <code>downloads.claude.ai</code> for Claude Code installed with its own installer, without cookies or credentials. These checks are on by default, and you can turn them off in settings. Installing an agent update always waits for you.</p>
        <p>Before creating a worktree from a named branch, OpenOrc runs your own Git to fetch that branch from the repository’s <code>origin</code> remote. The built-in Preview browser loads the pages you or an agent open, and keeps their cookies and site data in OpenOrc’s application data. Words you type in its address bar that are not an address are sent to Google as a search. If you turn on support for iOS simulators and Android emulators, OpenOrc installs its helper tools from npm with your own npm. The <a href={docHref("network")}>network page</a> lists every connection the app makes.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="cloud-title">
        <h2 id="cloud-title">OpenOrc Cloud</h2>
        <p>OpenOrc Cloud is an optional paid service run by OpenOrc. It runs threads on machines in the cloud, so they keep working while your Mac sleeps, and it holds what the people of an organization share. Nothing in this section applies until you sign in under Settings → Account.</p>
        <p>Your account holds your email address, your name, and a picture if you add one. Your teammates see the name and the picture. You sign in with a code that OpenOrc Cloud emails to you through Resend, an email service, which receives your address and the message. Each session records the device or browser it was made from, and a sign-in through the browser also records its IP address. The desktop app keeps its session encrypted in your operating system’s protected storage.</p>
        <p>Pro and extra cloud hours are paid for on a checkout page of Polar, the payment service. OpenOrc Cloud gives Polar your email address and an identifier for your account, and learns from Polar what you hold: your plan, your seats, and your hours. You enter your payment details on Polar’s page, under Polar’s terms.</p>
        <p>A cloud thread’s agent runs on a Linux machine that OpenOrc Cloud starts for you at a sandbox service, Vercel or Agent37. The machine holds the thread’s folder: its code, uncommitted changes, installed packages, and build output. Code reaches it from GitHub, through OpenOrc Cloud’s GitHub app and only for the repositories you or your organization chose, or from a folder on your Mac that has no remote, whose history the desktop app sends. If you connect GitHub, OpenOrc Cloud keeps the link to your GitHub account.</p>
        <p>An agent in the cloud runs on a sign-in you make for the cloud under Settings → Cloud. OpenOrc Cloud’s server keeps it sealed with AES-256-GCM, along with the headers of the connectors you set up for the cloud and the environment values of your projects. For a turn, the machine gets the sign-in of the person whose turn it is, and the agent’s provider receives the request and its context as it would from your computer. The logins your agents have on your computer never go to the cloud.</p>
        <p>The server keeps each cloud thread’s messages, notes, and the files sent to it, the thread’s saved work, which is its commits, its changes, small files that Git ignores, and the agent’s own record of the conversation, the skills you choose to send, the history of a project added from a folder on your Mac, and your organizations with their people, seats, team tasks, and Team chat with its files.</p>
        <p>Your own space is visible only to you. An organization’s cloud threads, and the files sent in them, are visible to everyone who can use their project, and Team chat conversations to their members. A teammate who writes in your thread runs their turn on their own sign-in and machine, unless their message joins a turn of yours that is already running. The <a href={docHref("security") + "#cloud"}>security page</a> covers who can read what, and <a href={docHref("storage") + "#cloud"}>data and storage</a> lists what is kept and for how long.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="iphone-title">
        <h2 id="iphone-title">The iPhone and iPad app</h2>
        <p>The same app runs on iPhone and iPad, and this section calls either one the phone. It works with the desktop app on your own Mac, and it never asks you to sign in.</p>
        <p>The app connects to the Macs you pair it with. Phone access is off until you turn it on in the desktop app’s settings. To pair, you scan the code your Mac shows or paste its link. The phone then connects straight to your Mac: over your local network when both are on it, or over your tailnet if you use Tailscale. Each connection is encrypted end to end with the Noise protocol and AES-GCM, using keys created on the phone and the Mac, so your router, Tailscale, and anyone else on the network cannot read or change what passes between them. While phone access is on, your Mac listens for phones on its network connections and accepts only the phones paired with it.</p>
        <p>If you are signed in to OpenOrc Cloud on a Mac the phone pairs with, that Mac gets the phone a session of its own for the same account. The phone keeps the session in the iOS Keychain and does not send it back to the Mac. With it, the phone talks to OpenOrc Cloud’s server directly, over HTTPS, so it reaches your cloud threads, your organizations, and Team chat while your Mac sleeps. What the OpenOrc Cloud section above says then applies to what you read and send there. If you sign out on the Mac, or someone else signs in there, the phone lets the session go.</p>
        <p>Everything the app shows comes from your Mac, or from OpenOrc Cloud for what lives there: your projects, threads, changes, and the Preview. What you send from the phone, including the photos you attach, is saved and run on your Mac like anything you do there, so the desktop app section above applies to it. Your agent’s provider receives a request you send from the phone just as it would from the desktop.</p>
        <p>For each Mac it pairs with, the phone keeps a private key in the iOS Keychain. The key stays on that device: it does not sync through iCloud Keychain and is not restored to another device. In its own storage the phone also keeps each Mac’s name, network addresses, and public key; your settings; your drafts of messages, commit messages, and pull requests; and copies of your project and thread lists and of the images in your threads, so the app can show them before your Mac answers.</p>
        <p>Your Mac keeps a record of each paired phone: the name iOS reports for it, its public key, when it was paired, and when it last connected. If you turn on notifications, the Mac also keeps the phone’s push token and your choice of alerts and sound. These records stay with the rest of OpenOrc’s data on your Mac.</p>
        <p>Notifications are optional and off until you allow them. iOS then gives the app a push token, which the phone sends only to the Macs it is paired with and, while it holds a cloud session, to OpenOrc Cloud’s server, which alerts you about your cloud threads in the same sealed way. To alert you, your Mac seals the notification’s text with a key that only your Mac and your phone share, using AES-GCM, and sends it to Apple’s Push Notification service for delivery. Apple sees the push token, when the alert was sent, the words “New activity on your Mac”, or “New activity in OpenOrc Cloud” for a cloud thread, and the sealed text. The phone unseals the text on the device, so Apple cannot read the thread name or what the alert says.</p>
        <p>The app uses the camera to scan the pairing code and to take photos you choose to attach. It reaches your photo library only through the iOS photo picker, which gives it just the photos you pick. It uses the local network to reach your Mac. It uses the microphone only while you dictate a message, from the moment you tap the microphone until you finish, cancel, or send. iOS turns what you say into text on the phone: the app asks for on-device recognition only, and does not dictate in a language the phone has no on-device model for. No audio is sent to your Mac, to OpenOrc Cloud, or to anyone else, and none is saved; the text is sent when you send the message, like anything you type. It never uses your location or your contacts. Photos you attach are resized on the phone and saved on your Mac, where your agent can read them.</p>
        <p>Besides your Macs and OpenOrc Cloud, the app makes two kinds of connection of its own. When it cannot reach your Mac while the phone is off Wi-Fi, it asks <code>www.apple.com</code> for the page Apple keeps for checking whether a device is online, so the app can tell you why your Mac is out of reach. That request carries nothing about you or your Mac. When a reply in a thread shows an image from the web, the phone loads that image from the site that hosts it, which sees your phone’s IP address, as it would in a browser. Links you open from a thread open in a Safari view. Visualizations that agents make run in a sandbox that cannot reach the internet.</p>
        <p>OpenOrc collects nothing from the iPhone and iPad app beyond what you read and send in OpenOrc Cloud once the phone holds a cloud session. The app has no analytics, advertising, tracking, or crash reporting of its own, and it does not follow you across other apps or websites. Apart from the images described above, nothing from the app reaches anyone but your Macs, OpenOrc Cloud, and Apple.</p>
        <p>Apple runs the App Store, TestFlight, and the push service under its own terms. If you test a build through TestFlight, Apple shares the information TestFlight describes with the developer, such as your name and email address, crash logs, and the feedback you send. If you choose to share analytics with app developers in your device’s settings, Apple may also pass along crash reports for the app.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="slack-title">
        <h2 id="slack-title">If you connect Slack</h2>
        <p>The optional Slack integration connects an activated Slack thread to a desktop conversation. Messages, participant and channel identifiers, progress updates, replies, and permission decisions pass between Slack and your computer. If you use a shared relay, the computer hosting it also routes these messages. Slack and your workspace administrator control the records kept in Slack. You can disconnect Slack in settings.</p>
        <p>Each request you send from an activated Slack thread reaches your provider with the thread’s earlier messages, including other participants’ messages and the images attached to them. OpenOrc also saves those messages in the desktop conversation.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="retention-title">
        <h2 id="retention-title">Storage and removal</h2>
        <p>Local records stay in OpenOrc’s application data until you remove them. The exception is the provider log in <code>logs/provider</code>, a copy of the agents’ raw output kept for troubleshooting: OpenOrc deletes its files after 14 days, or sooner once the folder passes 512 MB. Removing a local conversation does not remove copies held by providers, Slack, backups, or your project files. Uninstalling the app may leave its data folder in place.</p>
        <p>In OpenOrc Cloud, deleting a cloud thread deletes its conversation, subthreads, changes, saved work, and the files sent to it. Removing a cloud project deletes the project and its cloud threads, and leaves a folder on your Mac as it is. A thread’s saved work is otherwise kept for a time after the thread was last used, 30 days today, and a machine you stop using is removed. Removing a sign-in for the cloud deletes the server’s copy; revoke it with its provider to stop it working.</p>
        <p>You can delete your OpenOrc Cloud account under Settings → Account, after typing your email address and a code sent to it. It takes effect at once and cannot be undone. Your own space goes, with its cloud threads, projects, and saved work, and so do your cloud sign-ins, connectors, skills, and machines, your link to GitHub, your Pro, and any organization you are the only member of. In other organizations your place goes, and what you wrote there stays without your name. Threads on your computer stay, since they were never in OpenOrc Cloud.</p>
        <p>OpenOrc removes common secret formats, such as API keys, access tokens, passwords set in code or configuration, and credentials in URLs, from the records it keeps: execution events and their search index, run results and errors, the audit log, memories, summaries, and the provider log. It cannot recognize every sensitive value. Text that OpenOrc passes to an agent later, such as plans and task comments, is stored as written, and so are your unsent drafts.</p>
        <p>On the phone, removing a Mac deletes the phone’s key for it and the lists and images kept for it. Deleting the app removes its storage, though iOS can keep Keychain entries after an app is deleted. Removing a phone in the desktop app’s settings deletes its record and push token from your Mac, and any key the phone still holds stops working.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="contact-title">
        <h2 id="contact-title">Questions</h2>
        <p>Ask a privacy question or report a concern by opening an issue in the <a href="https://github.com/open-orc/openorc/issues">OpenOrc GitHub repository</a>. Issues are public, so do not include personal or confidential information.{supportEmail && <> You can also email <a href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>{supportEmail}</a>.</>} Requests about information held by a model provider, Slack, Apple, or another connected service should go to that service. The <a href="/support/">support page</a> lists other ways to get help.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="updates-title">
        <h2 id="updates-title">Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>This page changes when the website or one of the apps changes how it handles information. The date above shows the latest revision.</p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>
